      "math_academy_id": "e-11014",
      "problem": "If $\\sec{x} = -\\dfrac{11}{6},$ then $\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) =$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y = \\sec{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14263803/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sec{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\sec{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sec(-x) = \\sec{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sec{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x-14\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\sec{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) = -\\dfrac{11}{6}.$",
